\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied how a streaming respiratory sound detector can decide which of its expensive state updates to skip. Our model keeps a persistent graph of frequency regions, observes every chunk with a cheap encoder, and updates only a budget-limited set of regions chosen by a learned controller. The key change from AGN-Net is the training target of the controller: it predicts how much skipping a region's update would change the model's output, not how much the region's representation will change. On SPRSound, this controller preserves the full-update model's detections far better than random, periodic, energy-based, change-based and AGN-Net-style selection at every budget. With a quarter of the graph updates, its event F against the annotations is non-inferior to full updating. Analysis shows why: skip-harm utility is predictable from information available at inference time and concentrated in a few regions, whereas spectral change is a misleading signal. {{gg_disc}}

The work has limitations. All experiments use one paediatric dataset, whose test releases follow a different annotation style. Absolute detection scores are therefore low for every model, and they are below those of offline detectors built for this benchmark. The AGN-Net cell was not a better detector than a plain graph GRU. The end-to-end speedup was modest ({{rt_numpy_util@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 25\% of the updates) and appeared only in a lightweight runtime, since the controller itself costs about half of the time it saves. Finally, we did not compare with gates trained end to end on the task loss.

Future work includes a cheaper controller, multi-step utility targets, comparison with end-to-end trained gates, evaluation on other datasets and devices, and deployment on embedded hardware where sparse updates translate into energy savings. Code, data manifests, the patient-disjoint split and all per-recording results will be released with the paper.
